      "text": "To solve the equation \\(16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1\\), we start by expressing \\(16\\) as a power of \\(2\\). Since \\(16 = 2^4\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n(2^4)^{x^2 + y} + (2^4)^{x + y^2} = 1.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n2^{4(x^2 + y)} + 2^{4(x + y^2)} = 1.\n\\]\nSince \\(2^{4(x^2 + y)}\\) and \\(2^{4(x + y^2)}\\) are both non-negative, the only way their sum can be equal to \\(1\\) is if one of them is \\(0\\) and the other is \\(1\\). This is because the exponential function with a positive base is strictly increasing, and the only value at which the exponential function equals \\(1\\) is \\(0\\) and the only value at which the exponential function is \\(0\\) is when the exponent is \\(-\\infty\\). Therefore, we must have:\n\\[\n2^{4(x^2 + y)} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2^{4(x + y^2)} = 1.\n\\]\nThe first equation \\(2^{4(x^2 + y)} = 0\\) is never true for any real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) because the exponent \\(4(x^2 + y)\\) is positive for all real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Therefore, the second equation must be satisfied. The equation \\(2^{4(x + y^2)} = 1\\) implies:\n\\[\n4(x + y^2) = 0 \\implies x + y^2 = 0 \\implies x = -y^2.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(x = -y^2\\) into the equation \\(16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\n16^{(-y^2)^2 + y} + 16^{-y^2 + y^2} = 1.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n16^{y^4 + y} + 16^0 = 1.\n\\]\nSince \\(16^0 = 1\\), the equation further simplifies to:\n\\[\n16^{y^4 + y} + 1 = 1.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(1\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n16^{y^4 + y} = 0.\n\\]\nHowever, this is a contradiction because \\(16^{y^4 + y}\\) is always positive for all real numbers \\(y\\). Therefore, there are no real solutions to the equation. Thus, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) of real numbers that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]",
