      "text": "We begin by examining the equation \\(16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1\\). Since the right-hand side is 1 and the left-hand side is a sum of two powers of 16, both exponents must be equal to 0. This is because the only way a sum of two non-negative numbers can be 1 is if these two numbers are both 0.\n\nTherefore, we set up the following equations:\n\\[\nx^2 + y = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nx + y^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve these equations simultaneously. From the first equation, we can express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\\[\ny = -x^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(y = -x^2\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\nx + (-x^2)^2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nx + x^4 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nx(1 + x^3) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two possible solutions for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 1 + x^3 = 0\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x = 0\\), then substituting back into \\(y = -x^2\\) gives:\n\\[\ny = -0^2 = 0\n\\]\nSo, one solution is \\((x, y) = (0, 0)\\).\n\nIf \\(1 + x^3 = 0\\), then:\n\\[\nx^3 = -1\n\\]\n\\[\nx = -1\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(x = -1\\) into \\(y = -x^2\\) gives:\n\\[\ny = -(-1)^2 = -1\n\\]\nSo, another solution is \\((x, y) = (-1, -1)\\).\n\nThus, the ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) that satisfy the equation are \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((-1, -1)\\). Therefore, there are exactly two such pairs.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
